.Dd July 31, 2026
.Dt UTIME 1
.Os
.
.Sh NAME
.Nm utime
.Nd get or set utimes(2) at highest-available time resolution
.
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl alm
.Ar timestamp
.Ar files
.
.Nm
.Op Fl almn
.Ar files
.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
In the first form,
.Nm
sets the access and modification times of each
.Ar file
named on the command-line.
This behaves analogously to
.Xr touch 1 ,
except the timestamp is always specified as the first argument.
.
.Pp
In the second form,
.Nm
prints the modification time of each
.Ar file ,
prepending its name to the printed timestamp unless the
.Fl n
switch is set.
If the first
.Ar file
has a purely numeric name that could be confused for a timestamp,
prefix it with
.Sy ./
to avoid running
.Nm
in the first form, described previously.
.
.Pp
All timestamps used or displayed by
.Nm
are in seconds since the Unix epoch,
with a possible fractional component of varying precision.
.
.Pp
When the
.Fl l
switch is set,
symbolic links are not dereferenced when getting or setting their timestamps.
Note that setting symlink timestamps is not fully portable;
see the first entry under
.Sx CAVEATS .
.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Set the access and modification time of
.Pa frogurt.gif
to October 29, 1992 20:03:27.17140708 (EST):
.Dl Nm Sy 720407007.17140708 Pa frogurt.gif
.\" Simpsons reference, obvs. Timestamp shown is:
.\"   Original airdate of Simpsons S04E05
.\" + 8:00 PM Eastern (National FOX broadcast time)
.\" + timecode of this quote in an MKV rip
.\" + production code (9F04) in hex
.
.Pp
Print the modification times of
.Pa /var/log/system.*
.Dl Nm Pa /var/log/system.*
Output:
.Bd -literal -compact -offset 4n
/var/log/system.log: 1776238641.888238725
/var/log/system.log.0.gz: 1776175200.087771942
/var/log/system.log.1.gz: 1776088800.234045638
/var/log/system.log.2.gz: 1776002400.048894118
.Ed
.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr date 1 ,
.Xr stat 1 ,
.Xr touch 1 ,
.Xr clock_gettime 2 ,
.Xr utimes 2
.
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
was originally written as a small shell-function named
.Fn unixstamp
on October 15, 2020, \
designed to shim
.Xr stat 1
and call it with the correct arguments to display file modification times.
This 4-line shim was expanded to 26 in September 2023 when support for \
high-resolution timestamps was added,
along with an option to force the earlier behaviour of printing only \
low-resolution, seconds-based timestamps.
.Pp
On January 8, 2026, a separate shell-function for
.Em modifying
time timestamps was added,
named
.Fn mtime .
This was complementary to
.Fn unixstamp ,
and the two functions were frequently confused.
.Pp
On April 15, 2026,
both functions were replaced by the unified
.Nm
binary.
The option for
.Dq low-resolution
was tossed,
since authors who desire integral output can easily strip the \
fractional component using tools like
.Xr sed 1 .
Support for setting both access and modification times was added,
with options to limit operation to one or both properties.
To avoid needing to retrain muscle memory,
the command was written to enable the
.Fl a
and
.Fl m
switches by default when called as
.Nm atime
and
.Nm mtime ,
respectively.
.Pq although creation of these aliases is left as an exercise for the user .
.Pp
On July 31, 2026, the previously-undocumented
.Fl l
switch was detailed in
.Nm Ap s
manual page,
and support for systems whose
.Xr touch 1
implementation lacked support for the
.Fl h
switch was improved by falling back to the prefixed GNU variant,
when available,
and displaying a more helpful diagnostic message when not.
.
