.Sh CAVEATS
Modifying symlink timestamps is not supported on all platforms;
if
.Ql Nm touch Fl h
fails,
an attempt is made to use the GNU implementation instead
.Pq assumed to be named Nm gtouch .
If no such fallback exists,
or is installed under a prefix other than
.Dq g ,
.Nm
terminates with an error code before making any changes to the filesystem.
Note that
.Em reading
timestamps from symbolic links imposes no such portability issue.
.
.Pp
.de EM
.ie t .Em \\$1
.el      *\\$1*
..
Non-existent files are
.EM not
created when setting timestamps.
This is by design;
.Nm Ap s
intended purpose is to operate upon
.EM existing
files only.
To both create
.EM and
set the timestamp of an empty file,
users are advised to use the
.Xr touch 1
command first,
followed by
.Nm :
.Bd -literal -compact -offset indent
.Nm touch Pa ./new-file
.Nm Ar 1443581145.913283559 Pa ./new-file
.Ed
.
.Sh BUGS
Filenames aren't aligned vertically in list output.
.Pp
An option to modify the string separating filenames from timestamps is needed.
Something as simple as an
.Fl 0
switch for NUL-delimited output might suffice.
.
.Pp
Options for displaying inode change timestamps and birthtimes should be added.
Setting these fields to an arbitrary date appears to be impossible,
so an
.Fl b
and
.Fl c
option should be supported only when printing stats,
and issue an error if attempted to be used when setting utimes.
.
.Pp
Examples of
.Fl m
and
.Fl a
switch usage,
in both getter and setter operations,
should be added to this man page's
.Sx EXAMPLES
section.
